femto patch for hidreport 0.6.0
Subject: parser: Push/Pop save and restore global items only

Stack::push clones the local-item state (usages) as well as the globals, and
Stack::pop restores both. HID 1.11 section 6.2.2.7 defines Push and Pop as
operating on the global item state table; local items stay in effect until
the next Main item. Upstream carries a FIXME saying the clone is probably
wrong. With the upstream behaviour, usages declared between a Push and a Pop
are lost at the Pop.

Push and pop the globals only. Also reject a Pop of the initial global state
before mutating the stack (upstream popped first and checked afterwards).

This patch leaves an empty line where the removed block was, exactly as in
the vendored source.

Regression test in femto: crates/femto-remote/src/descriptor.rs.

--- a/src/lib.rs
+++ b/src/lib.rs
@@ -1316,29 +1316,17 @@
         let current = self.globals.last().unwrap();
         self.globals.push(*current);
 
-        // FIXME: this clones the Usage vector which is likely to mess us up, I don't
-        // think they transfer across push/pop
-        let current = self.locals.last().unwrap().clone();
-        self.locals.push(current);
     }
 
     fn pop(&mut self) -> Result<()> {
         ensure!(
-            !self.globals.is_empty() && !self.locals.is_empty(),
+            self.globals.len() > 1,
             ParserError::InvalidData {
                 offset: 0,
                 message: "Too many Pops".into(),
             }
         );
         self.globals.pop();
-        self.locals.pop();
-        ensure!(
-            !self.globals.is_empty() && !self.locals.is_empty(),
-            ParserError::InvalidData {
-                offset: 0,
-                message: "Too many Pops, not enough Pushes".into(),
-            }
-        );
         Ok(())
     }
 
